my musical training was the key to my  discovery it was not an engineering  discovery or a mathematical discovery it  was altogether an ear discovery in  autumn of dis of 1967 while  experimenting with two oscillators one  producing a vibrato in the second so  basically it's infrasonic FM only I  didn't call it FM it was just vibrato as  I increase the vibrato depth and at sub  audio rates and I could track the  instantaneous frequency as long as the  rate was not too great I increase the  depth and the rate to the point where I  could no longer track the instantaneous  frequency and then realized I was  hearing just this complex tone I decided  to increase both of these values around  a carrier this average frequency 200  Hertz from maybe the vibrato depth 200  Hertz so that would be from 0 to 400  this was a linear system of course and  then increasing the rate well so one  tries even numbers like 200 I'll try  5500 and 200 and then I thought you know  what I'm hearing is a complex tone that  seems to have be made of harmonics  that's what my ear was telling me I put  an envelope on the amplitude of the  modulating wave and of course I knew  that with zero amplitude I just got a  sinusoid and of course then I heard this  dynamic spectrum and my ear grabbed that  and understood immediately what was  going  although I didn't know the process I  knew that I heard that what I was  producing was something like a filter  being swept over a complex wave the  obvious thing to them well let's see if  this transposes so I tried the same  thing at 400 Hertz maintaining the  ratios then at 100 Hertz and then  realized that there was something  important here  brass tones had not been successfully  simulated I thought well if I'd use a  trumpet envelope on in a similar way as  the modulation index that would meet the  criteria that jean-claude had respect in  his simulations using no 10 or 15  oscillators that I had produced these  pretty believable trumpet like brass  like to